From 79e06ae89bbd3973b0384c82876a9fbf5af800f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florent Merlet <florentmerlet73@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:52:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] TIP 759 (1/7): expression substitution [(expr)]

In a word, "[(" starts an expression substitution: the expression runs
from that "(" to the ")" that balances it, directly followed by "]"
(")]" is one symbol). Its value replaces the substitution, as
[expr {...}] would. A command that is one expression substitution,
"[(...)]" alone, has the value of the expression as result (it is not
a command name).

- ParseTokens: TCL_TOKEN_SUB_EXPR token covering [(...)]; the end is
  found by the expression parser (TclParseExprSubst), so that strings,
  braces and nested substitutions are skipped correctly. The nesting
  depth is limited (interp recursionlimit).
- ParseExpr: [(...)] is also an operand of an expression.
- TclSubstTokens, TclCompileTokens (TclCompileSubExpr), [subst]
  compiler: evaluate/compile the text between the brackets as an
  expression.
- TclIsExprCommand: CompileCommandTokens and TclEvalEx take the value of
  a command "[(...)]" as its result.
- subst: new options -expr and -noexpr (TCL_SUBST_EXPR, in
  TCL_SUBST_ALL).
- Tests: exprSubst.test; subst.test messages. Docs: Tcl.n, expr.n,
  subst.n.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MrNWAEyXLqekovNBvbMZ4z
